        <h1>Effects of Microplastic Accumulation on Coral Reef Ecosystems: A Five-Year Field Study</h1>
        <p class="authors">R. Almeida, T. Nakamura, S. Okoro</p>
        <p class="affil">Department of Marine Biology, Coastal Research Institute</p>
        <h2>Abstract</h2>
        <p>Microplastic contamination in shallow reef environments has increased substantially over the past decade, yet
            its long-term ecological consequences remain poorly quantified. We conducted a five-year field study across
            twelve reef sites, measuring microplastic density, coral polyp stress response, and associated fish
            community composition. Sites with the highest microplastic concentrations showed a 34% reduction in coral
            recruitment and measurable shifts in polyp feeding behavior. Fish species richness declined most sharply
            among reef-dependent grazers, suggesting cascading effects beyond direct coral impact. These findings
            indicate that microplastic load is a meaningful, independent stressor on reef resilience, distinct from and
            additive to thermal bleaching pressure.</p>
        <h2>Keywords</h2>
        <p class="keywords">microplastics, coral reef ecology, marine pollution, reef resilience, benthic ecosystems</p>
